Subject: [PATCH] gl: serve the claimed GL limits on SwiftShader

On ANGLE's SwiftShader backend, getParameter returns the profile's
claimed limits, such as MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE, in place of the lower of claim
and driver. SwiftShader accepts allocations past its own reported
limits, so the lower number protects nothing, while a GeForce renderer
string beside a software limit table is an easy lookup for a page. The
whole claimed table is served, on the integer, 64-bit and float queries
alike. Hardware backends keep the lower of claim and driver, since they
reject work past what they report.

--- a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
@@ -296,6 +296,40 @@
      "MAX_TRANSFORM_FEEDBACK_INTERLEAVED_COMPONENTS"},
 };
 
+// True when the active backend enforces the limits it reports, so a claim
+// above one of them would be refused on first use.
+//
+// Measured through the shipped 152.0.7977.83 macOS artifact rather than
+// assumed, with texStorage2D / texStorage3D / renderbufferStorageMultisample
+// followed by a framebuffer-completeness check and a clear-and-readback:
+//
+//   ANGLE/Metal, reporting MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE 16384 and MAX_3D_TEXTURE_SIZE
+//   2048: 16384 allocates, is framebuffer-complete and reads back the colour
+//   it was cleared to; 32768 returns GL_INVALID_VALUE; a 3D texture at 4096
+//   returns GL_INVALID_VALUE; 8x multisample returns GL_INVALID_OPERATION.
+//   Every reported number is a real ceiling and every one is usable up to it.
+//
+//   ANGLE/SwiftShader: nothing errors, at any size, on any of the three. A 3D
+//   texture eight times the reported maximum is accepted. The numbers it
+//   reports are soft constants rather than hardware ceilings -- which is what
+//   patch 0027 established from the other direction when it raised three of
+//   them by editing the constant.
+//
+// So on a software rasteriser min() was giving up what serving a cluster is
+// for, and buying nothing with it. A page that reads MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE 8192
+// beside a GeForce renderer string needs no GL work at all to call that a
+// contradiction -- it is a lookup table -- whereas the over-claim it was
+// avoiding costs a detector a multi-hundred-megabyte allocation at the
+// reported maximum. The cheap check is the one to pass.
+//
+// SwiftShader specifically, not IsSoftwareGLImplementation: that also covers
+// D3D11 WARP, which is a different rasteriser whose enforcement has not been
+// measured here, and guessing in the permissive direction is how an
+// over-claim becomes a failing draw call.
+bool BackendEnforcesGlLimits() {
+  return GetGLImplementationParts().angle != ANGLEImplementation::kSwiftShader;
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
