-  // Serve the claimed device's limit, but never above what this driver can
-  // actually do. The clamp is the point: a limit is the one class of value a
-  // page falsifies by *using* it — allocate a texture past the reported size
-  // and an over-claim collapses in one call. Taking the minimum makes that
-  // impossible for any profile on any host, so a profile that is too ambitious
-  // for the machine it lands on degrades to the truth instead of lying.
+  // Serve the claimed device's limit. On a backend that enforces what it
+  // reports, never above what that driver can do: a limit is the one class of
+  // value a page falsifies by *using* it, so allocating a texture past the
+  // reported size collapses an over-claim in one call, and taking the minimum
+  // makes that impossible.
+  //
+  // On a backend that enforces nothing the minimum is the wrong rule, and this
+  // is not a corner case: the deployment target is a headless server with no
+  // GPU, where ANGLE runs SwiftShader. There, the reported number is the only
+  // thing a page can read, the claim is servable because the rasteriser will
+  // accept any size, and clamping leaves a discrete-GPU renderer string beside
+  // a software rasteriser's limit table -- which is a lookup, not a probe.
+  // BackendEnforcesGlLimits() carries the measurement that decides which.
   //
-  // This is the same rule SysInfo::NumberOfProcessors already follows for
-  // hardwareConcurrency, applied to the surface where getting it wrong is
-  // cheapest to detect.
+  // Serving the claim there is not free, and the cost is recorded rather than
+  // softened: a page that allocates at exactly the claimed maximum gets a
+  // framebuffer that is not complete and reads back black, where a real device
+  // returns what it was cleared to. That residual is the backend's, it is
+  // already present at the host's own reported maximum on this fork -- patch
+  // 0027 raised MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE from 8192 to 16384 and 16384 already fails
+  // completeness under SwiftShader -- and the reference product has it too.
   const base::apostate::Profile* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get();
   if (!profile || profile->gl_limits().empty() || !params) {
     return;
@@ -331,6 +376,10 @@
     }
